  <HOMEWORK title="DDR Bring-Up 5: Align DQS to CK - write levelling" prompt="CS/CA timing is trained. Now the memory test fails almost every word, and when a word is wrong the whole byte usually comes back looking unrelated to what was written - no single pin stands out. What training step aligns DQS to CK at the DRAM, and is it done yet?"
            hint="Check this chip's per-bit DQ deskew settings - are all eight bits skewed by about the same amount, or does one stand out?"
            explanation="All eight DQ bits are skewed by the same amount - a single, shared DQS-to-byte-lane timing error, exactly what write levelling exists to correct. Because every bit lags identically, the chip captures essentially the *previous* word's data for the whole byte rather than the current one - a uniform, whole-byte corruption, not an isolated-pin problem. Run write levelling (bring all eight DQ skews back to 0 together) before per-bit deskew in the next step - the identical real symptom as DDR Homework 7 in the flat set."/>
</LABCHAIN>
